# Project context for AI assistants (Cursor, etc.)

This is Nguyễn Quý Anh's ("Mason Nguyen") personal job-hunting portfolio/CV site. Built originally with Claude Code; this file exists so a fresh AI session in another editor doesn't have to re-derive project context from scratch.

## What this is

Two static, self-contained design versions of the same portfolio, sharing one content source:
- `/bold/` — **chosen as the primary version**, and the only one that got the 2026-09-03 IA/UX refactor below. The root `index.html` redirects here.
- `/minimal/` — light theme, restrained, Lenis smooth scroll + GSAP scroll reveals, custom cursor. Frozen at its 2026-08-29 structure (Hero → Clients → About → Experience → Projects → Competencies → Skills → Education → Contact) — kept for reference/comparison, not deleted, but nothing done to Bold since 2026-09-03 has been ported over. Ask the user before changing that.
- `/prototypes/` — throwaway wireframes used to review layout/copy before building the real thing into `bold/`. Not linked from the live site, and NOT kept in sync with it after approval — treat every file in here as a historical snapshot of what was pitched, not as current. `ownership-section.html` and `ia-refactor-plan.html` are the two so far.

### Bold's current section order (top to bottom) — 2026-09-03

`Hero → Clients → About → Ownership (positioning) → Projects (case studies) → Proof of Work (gallery) → Capabilities → Contact`

This is a deliberate storytelling order the user asked for explicitly (First impression → Competitive edge → Proof → Tech/operational capability → CTA) — don't reorder sections without that framing in mind. **Proof of Work sits inside the same "Proof" beat as Projects**, not a new one — that's why it got no new nav link. Nav is still 5 links: `about`, `ownership`, `projects`, `capabilities`, `contact` (no separate Experience/Skills/Education links — they live inside Capabilities now; Proof of Work is reached by scrolling or via the "Xem minh chứng dự án" button inside a project panel).

**Proof of Work** (`#proof`, Bold only) is a filterable masonry gallery fed by `DATA.proofOfWork.assets`, plus a modal (`#proofModal`) that opens a project-filtered subset of the same assets — triggered by a `[data-proof-project]` button that `renderProjectPanel()` only adds when `getAssetsForProject(proj.id)` is non-empty (so a project with zero proof assets gets no dead-end button). Assets can optionally sync live from a published Google Sheet instead of the mock data in `content.json` — see "Content architecture" and README.md §4 below for the mechanism and its one hard limitation (doesn't work over `file://`, by design — same CORS wall as everything else in that section).

**Projects is a tab bar + single panel, not stacked cards.** Only the active `DATA.projects.items[state.activeProjectTab]` is in `#projectPanel` at any time — the other four cases aren't in the DOM until their tab is clicked. `renderProjectPanel()` rebuilds the panel and must re-run `setupTiltCards()` every time (see footgun list) since it creates a fresh `.project-media` node.

**Capabilities is a bento merge of four previously-separate sections** (Experience, Core Competencies, Skills & Tools, Education) — `renderCapabilities()` in `bold/js/main.js` pulls from `DATA.experience`, `DATA.competencies`, `DATA.skills`, `DATA.education` and `DATA.capabilities` (just holds the section's own eyebrow/heading/labels, no items of its own). Experience renders as an accordion (`.cap-accordion-item`, one role open at a time, first role open by default) instead of always-expanded bullets — this is the main reason the section is short enough to share a screen with three other panels. If you add a 5th chapter/category, decide where it goes in the 2-column bento (`.cap-experience` spans both rows on the left; the other three stack on the right) rather than bolting on a 5th cell.

## Hard constraints — do not violate these

- **Vanilla HTML/CSS/JS only. No framework, no bundler, no build step.** The user is non-technical and must be able to open `minimal/index.html` or `bold/index.html` by double-clicking — no `npm install`, no dev server required to view the finished site.
- **Never use `fetch()` or `<script type="module">` for local files.** Both are blocked by CORS when a page is opened via `file://` (double-click), which is the primary way this site gets viewed. This is why content loads via a plain `<script src="../data/content.js">` tag instead of fetching JSON — see below.
- **Never fabricate CV content** (experience, numbers, clients, dates). All real content comes from the user's actual CV files; if something is missing or ambiguous, ask rather than invent.
- Animations must no-op under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` (already implemented — keep it that way if you touch animation code).

## Content architecture — read this before editing any text on the site

- `data/content.json` is the **single source of truth** for all bilingual (vi/en) text, both design versions render from it.
- `data/content.js` is a **generated mirror** (`window.SITE_CONTENT = {...}`) — this is what the pages actually `<script src>` load, because raw JSON can't be fetched under `file://`. It is NOT hand-edited.
- **After changing `content.json`, always run:**
  ```bash
  python3 scripts/build_content_js.py
  ```
  Forgetting this step means edits silently don't show up on the site — this is the single most common mistake when touching this project.
- `scripts/generate_cv_pdf.py` generates the two downloadable CV PDFs (`assets/cv/NguyenQuyAnh-CV-VI.pdf` / `-EN.pdf`) directly from `content.json`, using `fpdf2` + the system's Tahoma font (confirmed to render Vietnamese diacritics correctly — Helvetica/core PDF fonts do not). Re-run it after any content change so the downloadable CV stays in sync with the site.
- `clients.groups[].names` entries are objects, not plain strings: `{name, logo?, projectId?}`. `logo` is a relative path under `assets/images/logos/` (omit it for a client with no logo — the UI falls back to a text-only tile); `projectId` links the tile to a matching `projects.items[].id` so clicking it scrolls to that case study. Both `bold` and `minimal` read this same shape — if you add a client, update both versions' render functions only if you change the *shape* again, not for ordinary data edits.
- `proofOfWork.assets[]` (Bold only) is the gallery/modal's data — each entry is `{id, type, category, projectId, url, description}`. `type` is `image`/`pdf`/`video` (picks the tile icon); `category` must match one of `proofOfWork.categories[].id`; `projectId` must match a `projects.items[].id` for the case-study modal to find it. An entry with `url: ""` renders as an inert `.ph` placeholder tile (same convention as everywhere else missing media is handled in this project); a real `url` makes the tile clickable, opening that URL in a new tab — there's no in-page lightbox, deliberately, to keep scope sane.
  - `proofOfWork.sheetUrl` (default `""`) is an **optional live-data override**: if set, `loadProofAssetsFromSheet()` in `bold/js/main.js` fetches that Google Sheet's `gviz/tq?tqx=out:json` endpoint (no API key, no "Publish to web" step — just Share → Anyone with the link → Viewer) and replaces `state.proofAssets` with its rows if the fetch succeeds, re-rendering. If `sheetUrl` is blank, or the fetch fails for **any** reason — most commonly the page was opened via `file://` instead of a real deploy, which is exactly the fetch-from-file:// restriction documented in "Hard constraints" above — it silently keeps the mock `assets` from `content.json` and logs a `console.info` (never an error, never throws). Don't "fix" this by trying to make it work under `file://`; it structurally can't, and that's fine — see README.md §4 for what this means for the user's workflow.
- `ownership.chapters[]` (Bold only) mixes two layouts on purpose: a chapter with a `stat` field renders a stat-callout card instead of the generic `cases` placeholder grid (see `renderOwnership()` in `bold/js/main.js` — it branches on `chapter.stat`). A `cases[]` entry with `projectId` renders as clickable (scrolls to that project); without it, renders as an inert placeholder. Don't add a fourth chapter without deciding which layout it needs.

## Footguns already hit once — don't reintroduce any

1. **Never target `.stat-value` for anything other than the animated About counters.** `setupCounters()` does `document.querySelectorAll(".stat-value")` and expects every match to carry `data-count`/`data-prefix`/`data-suffix`. Reusing that class purely for its gradient-text look (e.g. the Ownership section's "50%") gets silently overwritten with the literal string `"null"` once the counter's fallback timer runs, since a missing attribute reads back as `null` and string-concatenates as text. If you want that visual treatment elsewhere, copy the CSS under a new class name (see `.ownership-stat-value`) — never reuse `.stat-value` itself.
2. **`setupLenis()` must drive Lenis from exactly one clock.** Calling `lenis.raf()` from both a manual `requestAnimationFrame` loop and `gsap.ticker.add()` at the same time feeds it two incompatible time epochs — `scrollTo()` then returns/resolves normally but the scroll never actually animates, with no error anywhere. Already fixed in both `bold/js/main.js` and `minimal/js/main.js` (GSAP's ticker drives Lenis exclusively whenever GSAP is loaded; the manual rAF loop is only a fallback for when it isn't). If you ever rewrite `setupLenis()`, keep that either/or — don't run both.
3. **`scrollToProject(id, lenis)` resolves by index into `DATA.projects.items`, not by querying the DOM.** It used to look for `.project-card[data-project-id="…"]`, which broke silently (no error, just did nothing) the moment Projects became tab-based and that class stopped existing. If Projects' markup changes again, remember this function — and the Clients-grid / Ownership case-card click-throughs that call it — needs to keep working by data lookup, not by assuming a specific card is sitting in the DOM.
4. **The `[data-proof-project]` button only exists for whichever project tab is currently active**, same single-panel-in-DOM reason as footgun 3 — `renderProjectPanel()` rebuilds `#projectPanel` per tab, so `document.querySelector('[data-proof-project]')` only ever finds the active tab's button. `setupProofModal()` correctly delegates its click listener on the persistent `#projectPanel` container rather than the button itself, so this isn't currently a bug — just don't write new code elsewhere that assumes more than one `[data-proof-project]` button exists in the DOM at once.

## Local dev server — use `scripts/dev_server.py`, not `python3 -m http.server`

Plain `http.server` sends no cache headers, and Chrome then caches responses on its own heuristics — edits can silently stop showing up on reload, with no way to tell from the page itself. Losing time to this is the single most likely way an editing session on this project goes sideways. Use:
```bash
python3 scripts/dev_server.py . 4173
```
(also wired up as the `portfolio-mason` launch config). If content still looks stale after that, it's not a cache problem — check the actual file and data flow.

## Resolved (do not reopen these without the user raising it first)

- Version choice: **Bold**, confirmed 2026-08-29. Root `index.html` redirects to `/bold/index.html`.
- Intern-mentoring count discrepancy between the two source CVs: confirmed as **9** (the EN CV's number) on 2026-09-02, overriding the earlier VI-CV-based guess of 8.
- Deploy: user asked for full auto-deploy with no further confirmation needed (2026-08-29). No hosting CLI (gh/vercel/netlify) was installed or authenticated on the machine this was built on, so it could not be completed end-to-end — Netlify Drop (no login required) was left as the one manual step. Check README.md / git remotes / recent commits for whether this has since been completed.
- 2026-09-02 revision batch (annotated directly in the review doc, not as native Word comments — diff the two versions of `Noi-dung-Portfolio-Mason.docx` if similar review docs appear again): years bumped 4→5 throughout, formal "Tôi" pronoun instead of "Mình", client logos + hover/click-through added to Bold's client grid, About photo layout made flexible for more images (user said 3-5 more coming later, not yet supplied), a 5th stat (ROAS) added — `about.highlights` and both versions' stats-grid CSS must stay `auto-fit`, not a fixed 4-column grid, if you touch that again.
- New "Ownership" positioning section (Bold only), added 2026-09-03: reviewed first as a standalone `prototypes/ownership-section.html` wireframe, approved by the user, then integrated for real into `bold/`. Uses "8 interns, 4 (50%) promoted" for its leadership stat — note this is a DIFFERENT figure than the "9 interns" used in the main Experience bullet (see the 2026-09-02 entry above). Both numbers came directly from the user across two separate messages and were flagged back to them as inconsistent; neither has been reconciled as of this writing — don't silently "fix" one to match the other without asking.
- Full IA/UX refactor of Bold, also 2026-09-03 (same day, later): user said the whole page felt oversized/overlong right after approving Ownership, asked for a full reorder + compaction in its style. Reviewed first as `prototypes/ia-refactor-plan.html` (a published Artifact + local copy), then built for real — see "Bold's current section order" above for the result. `minimal/` deliberately not touched.
- "Proof of Work" gallery + case-study cross-linking, also 2026-09-03 (same day, later still): user initially asked for this to auto-fetch from a Google Drive folder, optionally via React/Next.js. Two conflicts with the hard constraints above were flagged back to the user and resolved before building: (1) React/Next was declined in favor of staying Vanilla JS — the user's own phrasing already allowed either ("if using React/Next... or the equivalent Vanilla JS structure"), and switching frameworks now would've broken zero-build/`file://`-preview for the *entire* site, not just this feature; (2) Google Sheets was used instead of the Drive folder API — no API key/OAuth needed (a client-exposed Drive API key would be a real security/quota concern anyway), and a sheet's row/column shape maps directly onto the `{id, type, category, projectId, url, description}` schema this needed, where a Drive folder is just a flat file store with no metadata fields. Both were flagged to the user as the plan, not asked as an open question — see `proofOfWork.sheetUrl` above for the resulting mechanism, including its one accepted limitation (no live sync under `file://` — mock data renders instead, by design, not a bug to fix).

## Where to look

- `README.md` — Vietnamese, non-technical instructions for previewing locally and deploying (Netlify Drop / Vercel / GitHub Pages).
- `scripts/` — the Python helpers above; safe to re-run any time, all are idempotent.
